{
  "concept": "Because the speed of light is the same for every observer, the same two events (pulse emitted, pulse received) trace a short straight path in the shuttle frame but a longer diagonal path for an Earth observer, so the Earth observer must record a longer elapsed time — time dilation.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a): side silhouette of a space shuttle at top with a shaded 'zoom' wedge leading down to a rounded-rectangle magnified cabin inset",
    "Panel (a) inset contents: astronaut in orange suit floating at left with leader label 'Astronaut'; analog clock face with teal shading labeled 'Δτ'; yellow starburst 'Light source' and grey 'Receiver' box side by side at the bottom; small teal 'Mirror' bar at the top with label; teal arrow going straight up and a second teal arrow straight back down; vertical dimension bracket at right labeled 'D'",
    "Panel (b): three dashed-outline cabin snapshots in a row, each containing the mirror bar at top and the source/receiver/clock cluster at bottom",
    "Panel (b) light path: purple arrow segment labeled 's' rising diagonally from the source in the left snapshot to the mirror in the middle snapshot, second purple arrow labeled 's' descending to the receiver in the right snapshot",
    "Panel (b) labels: 'Beginning event' under left snapshot, 'Ending event' under right snapshot, horizontal measurement brackets 'L = vΔt/2' and 'L' between snapshot centers, vertical bracket 'D' inside the right snapshot",
    "Panel (b) observer: standing person on a tan ground plane labeled 'Observer on Earth' with leader arrow, plus analog clock labeled 'Δt'",
    "Panel (c): bare isosceles triangle — horizontal base line, vertical altitude labeled 'D', two slanted sides each labeled 's' with purple direction arrows, base halves labeled 'L = vΔt/2' and 'L', equation 's = √(D² + L²)' written above the apex",
    "Panel captions '(a)', '(b)', '(c)'",
    "Event markers (small glowing dots) at emission, reflection, and reception points, mirrored in both the apparatus panel and the triangle panel",
    "Readout box showing current v/c, Δτ, Δt, and the ratio Δt/Δτ = γ"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "frameToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The same physical apparatus gives a vertical 2D light path and a proper time Δτ in the astronaut's frame, but a longer diagonal 2s path and a larger Δt in the Earth frame — elapsed time depends on the observer's frame.",
      "label": "Observer frame",
      "options": ["Astronaut (in shuttle)", "Observer on Earth", "Both side by side"],
      "default": "Astronaut (in shuttle)"
    },
    {
      "id": "speed",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Increasing the shuttle's speed spreads the cabin snapshots apart, grows L, stretches the diagonal s, and widens the gap between Δt and Δτ — time dilation comes from geometry, not from clocks malfunctioning.",
      "label": "Shuttle speed v / c",
      "range": [0, 0.95, 0.01],
      "default": 0.6
    },
    {
      "id": "eventStep",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "Both observers agree on the three events themselves — emission, reflection, reception — and disagree only about the path length connecting them, so disagreement about time is not disagreement about what happened.",
      "label": "Step through events",
      "states": ["beginning event", "mirror reflection", "ending event"],
      "default": "beginning event"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The light clock at rest",
      "narration": "Inside the shuttle the astronaut sees the pulse go straight up to the mirror and straight back down, covering 2D. The onboard clock reads the proper time Δτ = 2D/c.",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Astronaut (in shuttle)", "speed": 0.6, "eventStep": "beginning event" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same events, Earth frame",
      "narration": "Switch to the Earth observer: the cabin has moved between emission and reception, so the identical pulse traces two diagonal segments s. The path is unmistakably longer than 2D.",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Observer on Earth", "speed": 0.6, "eventStep": "beginning event" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Walk the pulse through the three events",
      "narration": "Step from emission to reflection to reception. Each event lights up in the cabin snapshots and at the matching vertex of the triangle — both observers see exactly these three events.",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Observer on Earth", "speed": 0.6, "eventStep": "mirror reflection" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The ending event and the triangle",
      "narration": "At reception the diagonal path closes into the isosceles triangle with altitude D and base halves L = vΔt/2, so s = √(D² + L²).",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Observer on Earth", "speed": 0.6, "eventStep": "ending event" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slow the shuttle down",
      "narration": "At low speed L nearly vanishes, the triangle flattens toward a vertical line, and Δt collapses onto Δτ — the classical limit where everyone agrees on time.",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Both side by side", "speed": 0.1, "eventStep": "ending event" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push toward the speed of light",
      "narration": "Crank v up: the snapshots fly apart, s grows far beyond D, and Δt balloons relative to Δτ by the factor γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²).",
      "state": { "frameToggle": "Both side by side", "speed": 0.92, "eventStep": "ending event" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "If light must travel at c for both observers, then a longer light path can only mean a longer elapsed time — the Earth observer's diagonal hypotenuse is literally the extra time the moving clock 'loses'.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three panels arranged like the textbook: (a) top-left, (b) top-right, (c) bottom-center. Panel (a): grey/white shuttle silhouette above a pale-blue rounded-rectangle inset; orange-suited astronaut at left, teal mirror bar top-center, yellow starburst light source and grey receiver box at bottom-center, teal double-headed vertical path between them, black dimension bracket labeled D at the right, teal-faced analog clock labeled Δτ. Panel (b): three grey-filled dashed rounded rectangles in a row sharing the same internal layout, connected by purple arrows labeled s rising to the middle snapshot's mirror and falling to the right snapshot's receiver; horizontal brackets labeled 'L = vΔt/2' and 'L' below; a small standing figure in green/tan on a tan ground strip labeled 'Observer on Earth' with a clock labeled Δt. Panel (c): clean black isosceles triangle, purple slanted sides each labeled s with midpoint arrowheads, black vertical altitude labeled D, base halves labeled 'L = vΔt/2' and 'L', equation 's = √(D² + L²)' above the apex. Color code by frame: teal = astronaut/proper-time quantities, purple = Earth-frame path and times. Event markers are small glowing gold dots that appear simultaneously at the corresponding cabin position and triangle vertex when stepping. Keep a small numeric readout of v/c, Δτ, Δt and γ near panel (c). Snapshot spacing and triangle base width must scale directly with the speed slider; at v = 0 the triangle degenerates to a vertical line."
}

